USC: don't apply integer source modifiers twice on vector cores

GetSourceTypeless unpacks a channel of a vector register into a scalar for
integer instructions on SGX543/544.  It copied the source's negate/absolute
modifier onto the unpack and also returned it to the caller, so the modifier
was applied twice, the first time with floating point semantics (the unpack
became VMUL src, 1.0).  ExpandSUB turns every integer "a - b" into
"a + -b", so e.g. OpenCL get_group_id(0) = gid - offset computed
gid + (offset ^ 0x80000000).  Make the unpack a plain copy and leave the
modifiers to the caller.

Callers that cannot take modifiers still get an absolute modifier applied by
the unpack: the NZBIT flow control tests use it so that -0.0 tests as zero.
Their negate modifier inverts the test, which DoFlowControlTest already does,
so it is no longer also applied to the value (if (!x) on x = 0.0 tested
-0.0, i.e. non-zero bits).  Assert that no other such caller gets a negate.

diff --git a/tools/intern/usc2/icvt_core.c b/tools/intern/usc2/icvt_core.c
